## 1. Introduction
Water pollution caused by persistent organics is one of the most serious threats to human health [1,2]. Fenton-like reaction using peroxymonosulfate (PMS) as oxidant has been verified as an effective technique for their disposal [3]. Because of asymmetric structure and easy broken O-O bond, PMS can be activated to $S O_{4}^{-}$ and OH with the powerful oxidizability on transition metal surface, resulting in the non-selective pollutant degradation [4]. Among various transition metals, Co-based materials display the most excellent catalytic activity [5,6]. As a metal organic framework, Prussian blue analogue (PBA) is bridged by transition metals (such as Fe, Co, Ni and Mn) and cyanide ligands. In Fenton-like reaction, Co-based PBA possesses superior activity even than those of the frequently used cobalt oxides, owing to high surface area, tailorable porosity, and plentiful nanoscale channels [7]. However, one of the drawbacks for Co-based materials is that the leaching of cobalt ions strictly restricted due to high toxicity. The upper limit of the leached cobalt ions concentration is $1.0\:\mathrm{mg/mL}$ according to the China Emission Standard [8–10].
To address this question, core@shell structure is constructed to suppress the leaching with the help of protective shell on the surface of Co-based materials [11,12]. For example, the concentration of the leached cobalt ion was reduced by half after coating poly (mphenylenediamine) on PBA surface [11]. The leached cobalt ion concentration from FeCo@nitrogen-doped graphitized carbon core@shell structure was only one-tenth of that from the pure FeCo alloys [13,14]. Nevertheless, it is unable to maximally exploit the active sites on the core surface due to the close contact of shell [15,16]. Therefore, more and more attentions are paid to the yolk@shell structure, since yolk@shell structure not only inherits the protective shell from the core@shell structure, but also fully exposes the active sites due to the existence of hollow cavity [17–19]. Moreover, the yolk@shell structure is usually employed as a nanoreactor for various chemical reactions by taking advantage of the well-known ‘‘confinement effect” inside the cavity, in which both the reaction rate and selectivity are enhanced [20]. For example, Zeng et al. have confirmed that $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ metalorganic-framework yolk@shell nanoreactor displayed better degradation performance than that of the core@shell counterparts in Fenton-like reaction [19]. Similarly, the degradation rate of ${\mathsf{C o@C}}$ yolk@shell nanoreactor was higher than that of the solid $_{\mathrm{Co/C}}$ composites [17].
Besides high reactivity and low leaching, the recyclability of catalysts is another important issue in heterogeneous Fenton-like reaction, since it determines the application cost and secondary pollution [21–23]. For nanocatalysts, their separation and recovery are not easy owing to the ultra-small size. Among various separation technologies, magnetic separation is the most effective one [24,25]. Unfortunately, unlike the cobalt oxides, the Co-based PBA does not possess the superparamagnetic property. Therefore, the centrifugation or filtration were the commonly used separation approaches in early studies, leading to a low efficiency [24]. How can we endow the yolk@shell structure with superparamagnetic property without sacrificing high reactivity and suppression effect? Confining both $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ and Co-based PBA nanoparticles inside a protective shell and constructing ‘‘double-yolk egg-like” structure is a feasible solution, since multiple functionalities are integrated in this special structure without interference: The protective effect is from the external shell, the magnetic property is from $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles, and the high catalytic activity toward PMS is from the PBA core. Apparently, these multiple functionalities can not be provided by traditional yolk@shell structure with only one yolk.
Based on above statements, in this paper, a ‘‘double-yolk egglike” $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{P B}}{\mathsf{A@}}$ polypyrrole (PPy) nanoreactor was prepared, in which both $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ and Co-based PBA were encapsulated in PPy shell. First, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ yolk@shell precursor was prepared by a hard-template assisted etching method [26]. Then, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanosphere was employed as a reactive-template for PBA. By controlling the etching time and HCl concentration, both $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ and PBA could be simultaneously encapsulated into the PPy shell, leading to a double-yolk egg-like structure. The formation mechanism was carefully investigated. In PMS-based Fenton-like reaction, tetracycline (TC) was rapidly degraded within $15\ \mathrm{min}$ , and the degradation process was carefully optimized. The confinement effect inside the nanoreactor, suppression effect on the leaching, and the magnetic recyclability were illustrated by radical trapping experiment, electronic paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectrometer, inductively coupled plasma (ICP) atomic spectroscopy, and recycling experiment. Compared to traditional yolk@shell structure, Although double-yolk egg-like structure was an important extension for traditional yolk@shell structure and possessed more functionalities, to the best of our knowledge, seldom papers reported their preparation due to complex structure. In this work, we not only provided a feasible way for their synthesis, but also explored advantages in Fenton-like reaction, such as high reactivity from the confinement effect, low leaching by the suppression effect from the protective shell, and magnetic recyclability through introduction of another functional yolk. Therefore, it will inspire researchers to design other double-yolk egg-like nanoreactor with multiple functionalities.
## 2. Materials and method
## 2.1. Materials
Pyrrole monomer was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich, and it was distilled under the reduced pressure and stored at $-4^{\circ}C$ prior to use. $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]$ , $\mathsf{N a O H}$ , HCl, NaCl, ${\mathsf{N a C l O}}_{4}$ , ${\mathsf{N a N O}}_{3}$ , ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ , ${\sf N a H}_{2}{\sf P O}_{4}$ , ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ , ${\mathsf{N a H C O}}_{3}$ , trisodium citrate, ethanol, ethylene glycol, tetraethyl orthosilicate (TEOS), sodium acetate (NaAC), peroxymonosulfate (PMS), ammonium hydroxide $(\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $25.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , tetracycline (TC), poly(Nvinylpyrrolidone) (PVP), humic acid (HA), tert-butyl alcohol (TBA), 2,2,6,6-tetramethyl-4-piperidino (TEMP), and 5,5- Dimethyl-1-pyrroline N-oxide (DMPO) were purchased from China Pharmaceutical Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. All chemicals were analytical grade and used as received. The deionized water was purified through a Millipore system with a resistivity of $18.2\mathrm{M}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .
## 2.2. Preparation of yolk@shell nanoreactor
First, the sandwiched $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{PPy}$ composite was synthesized based on our early work (Text S1) [26]. Then, $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ middle layer was selectively etched by $5.0~\mathrm{M}$ of NaOH solution for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ yolk $@$ shell precursor was prepared. Subsequently, $1.2{\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]$ was added into $5.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ solution $(40\mathrm{mg/mL})$ under stirring for $24\mathrm{h}$ . After separation by magnet, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ precursor adsorbed with $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]^{3-}$ was placed in vacuum oven for $30\mathrm{min}$ , and then re-dispersed into $2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of HCl solution $(12.0~\mathrm{M})$ . Next, the reaction was proceeded for a certain time, and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y-T}$ nanoreactors with double-yolk were prepared, where T represented the reaction time. Further extending the reaction time to 60 or 240 min, PBA@PPy-60 or PBA@PPy-240 yolk@shell nanoreactor were obtained. Finally, the as-prepared nanoreactors were washed by deionized water for 3 times, and then dried under the ambient temperature.
## 2.3. Fenton-like reaction toward tetracycline degradation
## 2.3.1. Heterogeneous Fenton-like reaction
In a typical experiment, $5.0\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst was added into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of TC solution $\left(30~\mathrm{mg/L}\right)$ and stirred for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ to establish the adsorption-desorption equilibrium in the darkness. Then, $5.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of PMS was added into the suspension under stirring to start the Fenton-like reaction. Every $3.0\ \mathrm{min}$ , $3.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of the solution was sampled. After magnetic separation, $2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of supernatant was extracted to determine the TC concentration on a UV–Vis spectrophotometry according to the Lambert-Beer law. All experiments were performed at least 3 times with a relative error less than $5.0\%$ .
## 2.3.2. Homogeneous Fenton-like reaction
After heterogeneous Fenton-like reaction, the catalysts were removed via filter membrane with pore size of $0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Then, the filtrate was heated at $90~^{\circ}C$ for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ to quench the residual radicals. Next, TC was re-added into the filtrate, and their concentration was the same as that of the original solution $(30~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , $50~\mathrm{mL}$ ). Finally, $5.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of PMS was added under stirring to start the homogeneous Fenton-like reaction.
## 2.3.3. Recycled experiment
After Fenton-like reaction, the catalyst was separated by magnet, and washed by deionized water, and then dried in an oven at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ . The catalyst was reused in the next cycle under the identical condition.
## 2.3.4. Radical trapping experiment
In radical trapping experiment, the procedure for Fenton-like reaction was similar to the typical one (Section 2.3.1), except that MeOH $20~\mathrm{mM}.$ , scavenger of $S O_{4}^{-}$ and OH), TBA $20\mathrm{mM}$ , scavenger of OH), and ${\mathsf{N a N}}_{3}$ $1.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ , scavenger of $^10_{2}$ ), were respectively added into the solution before initiating the Fenton-like reaction.
## 2.4. Characterization
The crystal phases of catalysts were characterized by X-ray diffraction (XRD) using a Smartlab apparatus $\mathrm{\DeltaCu_{K\propto}}$ radiation, $\lambda=$ 1.5418 Å, $2\theta=10{-}40^{\circ},$ . Fourier transform infrared spectrometer (FTIR) was employed to determine the functional groups on sample surface (Nicolet Avatar 360). The morphology and microstructure were characterized by scanning electron microscope (SEM, Hitachi S4800) and transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEOL JEM2100). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was carried out on a VG ESCALAB MKII X-ray photoelectron spectrometer with $\mathrm{Mg}_{\mathrm{K}\upalpha}$ excitation $(1253.6\ \mathrm{eV})$ . Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) specific surface area was obtained by measuring $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorptiondesorption with a Beckman Coulter 3100 pore size and surface area analyzer. Inductively coupled plasma (ICP) atomic spectroscopy was performed on an Optima 7000 DV. The mineralization of TC was checked by a total organic carbon analyzer (TOC-VCPH/CPN, Shimadzu, Japan). Electronic spin resonance (ESR) spectrometer was applied for investigation of the active radicals (Bruker A200 spectrometer). The degradation process was monitored on UV– Vis spectrophotometry (Labtech UV9100B).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structural characterization
Fig. 1 shows the synthetic procedure of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y}$ and PBA@PPy nanoreactors. Firstly, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ yolk@shell precursor was synthesized according to a classical hard-template assisted etching method. The $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]^{3-}$ was diffused into the hollow cavity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ under the vacuum drying. After adding HCl, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ core was served as a reactive-template and gradually etched. The released $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ were immediately reacted with $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]^{3-}$ for PBA nanocubes (Eqs. (1) and (2)) [27], and the double-yolk egglike $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y}$ nanoreactor was prepared. Further extending the etching time, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ core was completely removed, leading to the formation of PBA@PPy nanoreactor.
$$
\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}+8\mathrm{H}^{+}\rightarrow\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+2\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\mathrm{/Fe}^{3+}\ +\ \mathrm{/Co(CN)}_{6}]^{3-}\ \longrightarrow\mathrm{KFe}\ \left[\mathrm{Co(CN)}_{6}\right]/\mathrm{Fe}\ \left[\mathrm{Co(CN)}_{6}\right]\left\{\right.
$$
The preparation process of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}\mathrm{yolk}@\mathrm{s}$ hell precursor was characterized by SEM and TEM measurements. $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanosphere is covered by a smooth $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ shell with an average thickness of $150\ \mathrm{nm}$ , leading to the formation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathsf{S i O}}_{2}$ core@shell composite (Fig. 2a and 2d). After coating $95\ \mathrm{nm}$ of PPy shell, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{PPy}$ sandwiched composite with a rough surface is prepared (Fig. 2b and 2e). $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ yolk@shell precursor is obtained via selectively etching the middle $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ layer by NaOH solution (Fig. 2c and 2f). The hollow cavity provides an ideal environment for subsequent chemical reaction.
The double-yolk egg-like $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y}$ nanoreactor was prepared via a reactive-template method inside $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ . When the etching time increases from 0.0 to $50~\mathrm{min}$ (Fig. 3a-3f), the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanospheres become looser and looser, and their diameters are gradually reduced. The PBA nanocubes are witnessed at $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , and their side-lengths become larger and larger, leading to a ‘‘double-yolk” structure. The unfilled space is able for Fenton-like reaction, therefore, $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y-T}$ can be functioned as an ideal nanoreactor. The relationship between the etching times and the sizes of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ nanospheres and PBA nanocubes are plotted in Fig. 3i (Table S1). By taking advantage of superparamagnetic property from the residual $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ yolk, $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y-T}$ can be rapidly separated from the aqueous solution by a magnet (Fig. S1). After $60\ \mathrm{min}$ , only PBA nanocubes are encapsulated in the PPy shell, while $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanospheres are invisible, suggesting the complete removal (Fig. 3g). In elemental mapping, Fe and Co are homogeneously dispersed in the yolk region and overlapped with each other, further confirming the formation of PBA (Fig. 2i and 2j). Further extending the reaction time to $240\ \mathrm{\min}$ , little structural changes are observed (Fig. 3h). In this case, PBA@PPy-60 and PBA@PPy-240 can not be magnetically separated (Fig. S1).
XRD characterization is employed to analyze the crystal phase of yolk@shell nanoreactor at different etching times. For pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ , the main diffraction peak at $2\theta=35.7^{\circ}$ is indexed to the (311) plane (JCPDS NO. 75–0449). For pure PBA, four peaks at $2\theta=17.2\$ , 24.4, 34.8, and $39.1^{\circ}$ are corresponding to the (200), (220), (400), and (420) planes of PBA (JCPDS NO. 89–3736), respectively [28]. It is obvious that the peak intensities of PBA gradually increase during the period of $10{-}50~\mathrm{min}$ (Fig. 4a), suggesting the increased PBA content. This is consistent with the observation in TEM images (Fig. 3b-3f). By magnifying the (200) plane, it is found that the peak position shifts to large angle in comparison with the standard card of PBA, due to the incorporation of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ with small atomic radius into the crystal lattice of PBA (inset of Fig. 4a) [29].
Fig. 4b shows the FTIR spectra of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ , $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y-}$ 10 and PBA@PPy-60. The absorption peaks at 527 and $2164~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are corresponding to $\scriptstyle\mathtt{F e-C}=\Nu-C0$ and $\mathsf{C}\equiv\mathsf{N}$ bonds in PBA, respectively [28]. It is worth noting that the intensities of these two peaks gradually increase with the extension of the etching times, and this is in a good agreement with the XRD patterns. In addition, seven absorption peaks assigned to PPy remain unchanged (blue dotted line) [26], suggesting the $\mathsf{P P y}$ shell is well preserved during the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ etching process.
From Fig. 3b-3h, it is evident that the growth of PBA is greatly restrained by the limited space inside the PPy shell. The average side-length of PBA in Fig. 3g was about $360\mathrm{nm}$ , much smaller than $410\ \mathrm{nm}$ of PBA for free growth using pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanosphere as reactive-template (Text S2 and Fig. S2a). The PBA in these two SEM views are represented cube-like morphology, indicating that the PPy shell is not a crucial factor for shaping PBA structure. Nevertheless, the cavity size is important to maintain the integrity of yolk@shell structure. Once the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ precursor with cavity size of $200~\mathrm{{nm}}$ is served as the reactive-template, the PPy shell is burst by PBA nanocubes (Fig. S3). In another control experiment, $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ are simply added into the ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}[{\mathrm{Co}}$ $(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]$ solution, and the PBA nanocubes are also prepared, indicating that the shape of PBA is independent of reactive-template method (Text S2 and Fig. S2b).
The formation mechanism of double-yolk egg-like $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/$ PBA@PPy nanoreactor is required to be revealed. In preparation process, HCl solution concentration is as high as $12.0\ \mathrm{M}$ and the space of hollow cavity is limited, leading to the high supersaturation of the released $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ inside the PPy shell. In Eq. (3), $\mu_{\mathrm{l}}$ and $\mu_{\mathrm{c}}$ are the chemical potentials of solute in solution and solid crystal, respectively. $\sigma$ is the specific surface energy of crystallites, $\upsilon$ is the volume of a single growth unit, and $h$ is the distance from the center of crystallite to its surface [30,31]. According to the Eq. (3), the specific surface energy of crystallites $(\sigma)$ is positive proportion to the supersaturation of the growth units $(\Delta\mu)$ [30,31]. For PBA, the surface energy of crystal planes follows the order: $(100)>(111)>(110).$ Once (100) plane governs the crystal growth, the nanoparticles will exhibit a cube-like morphology [32]. Besides affecting the PBA shape, the supersaturation of the solution also determines the nucleation mechanism. Under the high supersaturation, the crystal nucleus is preferred to nucleate homogeneously in the solution rather than nucleate heterogeneously on the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ surface. This is confirmed by the appearance of double-yolk or even triple-yolk (Fig. 4b-4f and 5a). Therefore, we inferred that both the cube-like morphology of PBA and the double-yolk structure could be controlled by the concentrations of reactants inside the PPy shell. For verification, the concentration of HCl solution is reduced to $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ , and the concentration of released $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ inside the PPy shell is largely lowered due to the slow etching rate of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ nanospheres. In this case, the asprepared PBA prefers to nucleate heterogeneously on the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ surface, leading to a well-defined core@shell structure (Fig. 5b). Compared to the samples in Fig. 5b, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}-10$ was preferred due to the superior reactivity (see below).
$$
\Delta\mu=\mu_{\mathrm{l}}-\mu_{\mathrm{c}}=2\sigma\nu/h
$$
## 3.2. Catalytic performance
The degradation performance of nanoreactor is evaluated using TC as target pollutant (Fig. 6a). Before initiating the Fenton-like reaction, the catalysts are stirred in TC solution for $60\mathrm{{min}}$ to establish adsorption-desorption equilibrium, and their adsorption efficiencies are around $10.0\pm2.0\%$ , suggesting the slight difference in adsorption between different catalysts. After adding PMS, only $7.4\%$ of TC is removed within $15\mathrm{min}$ . By using $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ as catalyst, the degradation efficiency almost remains unchanged, indicating the poor catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanospheres. For contrast, the degradation efficiencies remarkably increase to $77.2\pm6.6\%$ using $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y-T}$ , suggesting the PBA plays a key role for the enhancement of degradation performance. This is consistent with the early reports that Co possesses the best catalytic activity among various elements [33,34]. Little changes in degradation efficiencies are observed using PBA@PPy-60 and PBA@PPy-240 as catalysts. It is necessary to mention that the degradation efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ is higher than that of samples in Fig. 5b $(72.4\%$ orange curve in Fig. 6), due to full exposure of active sites.
During the degradation, the metal ions inevitably leach into the solution and initiate a homogeneous Fenton-like reaction. To determine the contribution, the reaction solution after removing the catalyst via centrifugation was directly used for Fenton-like reaction by re-adding PMS and TC. As shown in Fig. 6b, the degradation efficiency is only $13.6\pm8.0\%$ , indicating the limited contribution of homogeneous Fenton-like reaction. Therefore, the heterogeneous Fenton-like reaction played the dominating role for TC degradation.
The pseudo first-order kinetics and pseudo second-order kinetics were selected to simulate the degradation process, and the latter is more suitable than the former, due to the higher $R^{2}$ value (Fig. S4). This is contradiction with some previous works, since pseudo first-order kinetics was commonly preferred. We have studied the relationship between $k$ values and pollutant concentrations. As shown in Fig. 7a, although the degradation efficiency is only reduced by $14.5\%$ during the TC concentration from 20 to $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , the degradation rates largely drops by almost half (Inset of Fig. 7a and Table S2). This provided another evidence that the pseudo second-order kinetics is a better choice, due to the remarkable influence of initial pollutant concentration on degradation rate [35].
Table S3 lists the $k$ values of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y-T,}$ in which the $k$ value of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PB}\mathrm{A@\mathrm{PP}}\mathrm{y}-10$ is 63.3 times than that of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@$ - PPy precursor. In this study, $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}-10$ is selected as a standard sample, owing to the most rapid degradation rate (0.38 $\mathsf{L}{\cdot}\mathsf{m i n}{\cdot}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ ), low contribution of homogeneous reaction $(8.4\%)$ , short preparation time $\left[10\mathrm{min}\right]$ , and especially good superparamagnetic property. The influence of PMS dosage on degradation efficiency was studied. When the PMS dosages increase from 2.5 to $20~\mathrm{mg}$ (Fig. 7b), the TC removal efficiencies are promoted at the beginning (from 2.5 to $5.0~\mathrm{mg}$ ), and then remain at $74.3\%$ with a slight fluctuation (from 10 to $20\ \mathrm{\mg}$ ). At initial stage (2.5 to $5.0~\mathrm{mg}^{\cdot}$ ), there were a large number of unoccupied active sites on the catalyst surface for activation of the increasing PMS, leading to the higher yield of radicals and better degradation performance. However, with the increase of dosage, the overused PMS could not be immediately activated by the limited active sites. Meanwhile, the excessive ${\mathrm{HSO}}_{5}^{-}$ may be captured by $S O_{4}^{-}$ for $S0_{5}^{\cdot-}$ with low oxidability, leading to the slight increase of degradation performance [36,37].
To reveal the confinement effect on acceleration of degradation process, a reference $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A}{-}10$ without of PPy shell was prepared (Text S2). From the SEM image, we can see that the PBA nanocubes and residual $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanospheres are physically mixed together (Fig. S5). Under the identical degradation condition, their degradation efficiency was $51.3\%$ , and the $k$ value is 0.074 L min $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ . The degradation rate of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PB}\mathrm{A@\mathrm{PPy}}-10$ is 5.1 times than that of the reference $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A}{-}10$ (Fig. S6a). Furthermore, TOC data were measured to evaluate the mineralization performance. Compared to $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{PBA}\mathrm{-}10\ (27.2\%)$ , the value of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y-}$ 10 increases to $64.7\%$ , suggesting that most of TC are mineralized to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ .
The only structural difference between $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{PBA}/-10$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ is whether existence of PPy shell. Although
PPy shell does not possess the catalytic activity (red curve in Fig. S6a), the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ still shows better activity than that of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A}{-}10$ , the enhancement of degradation performance is attributed to the confinement effect inside the yolk@shell nanoreactor. As shown in Fig. S7, the surface area of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/$ PBA@PPy-10 is $23.5\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ , and the pore distribution mainly ranges from 3.0 to $5.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The sizes of TC $\cdot1.25~\mathrm{nm})$ and PMS $(1.16~\mathrm{nm})$ 1 are smaller than the pore size, ensuring easily diffuse into the hollow cavity and enrich there. Once the PMS is activated to be $S O_{4}^{-}$ and $\cdot_{\mathrm{{OH}}}$ , both the concentrations of these radicals and TC are ultra-high due to the limited space in cavity, leading to the better degradation performance.
Besides the confinement effect, the PPy shell also provides the protective function to reduce the leaching of metal ions. Herein, the concentrations of the leached iron and cobalt ions from $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A}{-}10$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ are measured by ICP analysis, and the values are 0.23 and $0.17~\mathrm{mg/L}$ for $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ , and 0.70 and $1.47~\mathrm{mg/L}$ for $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{PBA}/-10$ , respectively. These data clearly prove the positive function of PPy shell in suppression of leaching and avoidance of the secondary pollution. The contribution of the homogeneous Fenton-like reaction initiated by these leached ions were studied. As shown in Fig. S6b, only $15.8\%$ of TC is degraded by leached ions from $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ , while the degradation efficiency is 2.3 times higher by the leached ions from $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{PBA}/-10$ .
## 3.3. Catalytic mechanism
The existence of possible active species, such as $S O_{4}^{-}$ , OH and ${}^{1}0_{2}$ , are verified by ESR measurement. As shown in Fig. S8, the signals of $\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{-}$ and OH are visible using DMPO as trapping agent. Besides, the characteristic triplet peaks with the intensity ratio of 1:1:1 is assigned to TEMP- $^10_{2}$ in Fig. 8a. The contributions of these active species are determined by radical trapping experiment. MeOH is usually employed as the scavenges of both $S O_{4}^{-}$ and OH, while TBA mainly quench OH. Compared to the blank solution $(83.5\%$ of degradation efficiency), the degradation efficiency is reduced by $56.7\%$ and $18.5\%$ in the presence of MeOH and TBA, respectively (Fig. 8c). After adding $\mathsf{N a N}_{3}$ (scavenger of $^10_{2}$ ), the removal efficiency of TC is decreased by $63.8\%$ . Therefore, $S O_{4}^{-}$ , OH and $^10_{2}$ play a joint role for TC degradation, and $^10_{2}$ makes the highest contribution in Fenton-like reaction using $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/$ PBA@PPy-10 as catalyst.
The similar radical trapping experiment is also carried out in Fenton-like reaction employing $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A}{-}10$ as catalyst. $17.3\%$ $1.2\%$ and $12.5\%$ of the decreased efficiency using MeOH, TBA and ${\mathsf{N a N}}_{3}$ as scavenger indicates that the main radical is OH rather than $^10_{2}$ (Fig. 8d). As mentioned above, PPy shell is incapable of PMS activation. Therefore, the active sites on $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y-}10$ are similar to $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A}{-}10$ . A question is raised, why the main active species are different in these two systems? As commonly known, $^10_{2}$ was not directly produced via PMS activation, and their source should be traced. Compared to PMS alone, the signal intensity only slightly increases after adding $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{PBA}{-}10$ , while dramatically increases after adding $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ (Fig. 8a). This suggests that $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ possesses much better activity for $^10_{2}$ production, which is in a good agreement with the radical trapping experiment. According to Eqs. 4 and 5b [8,36], only $S O_{4}^{-}$ and OH are directly generated in PMS-based Fenton-like reaction. Therefore, TBA and MeOH are added in ESR measurement. In the presence of TBA (scavenger of OH), the signal intensity of TEMP- $^10_{2}$ is largely reduced in comparison with the blank solution (red curve in Fig. 8b), suggesting that OH serves as the raw materials for $^10_{2}$ (Eq. (6)). Once the MeOH (scavenger of both $S O_{4}^{-}$ and OH) is added, the signal of TEMP- $^10_{2}$ is almost invisible, revealing $S O_{4}^{-}$ also serves as the raw material for $^10_{2}$ (blue curve in Fig. 8b,
Eqs. (6) and (7)) [38]. Nevertheless, another question is raised. $S0_{4}^{-}$ and OH are also produced by $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{PBA}{-}10$ , why $^10_{2}$ does not play a dominating role? Let us turn attention to the structure of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/$ PBA-10 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy-}10.$ Once the radicals are produced on the surface of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{PBA}/-10$ , they will rapidly diffuse to the solution, leading to a low concentration. For contrast, radicals are produced inside $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}-10$ nanoreactor, and the local concentrations of these radicals are ultra-high due to the limited space in cavity. According to the principle of chemical equilibrium, it is beneficial for high yield of $^10_{2}$ (Eqs. (6) and (7)). Therefore, $^10_{2}$ acts as the main active species, and an outstanding degradation performance is realized (Fig. S6a).
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{Co^{2+}+H S O_{5}^{-}\to C o^{3+}+S O_{4}^{-}+\cdot^{\cdot}O H}}\\ &{\mathrm{Fe^{2+}+H S O_{5}^{-}\to F e^{3+}+S O_{4}^{-}+\cdot^{\cdot}O H}}\\ &{\mathrm{2\cdotOH\to^{1}\ O_{2}+H_{2}O}}\\ &{\mathrm{SO_{4}^{-}+H_{2}O\to S O_{4}^{2-}+\cdot^{\cdot}O H+H^{+}}}\\ &{\mathrm{SO_{4}^{-}/\ O H/^{1}O_{2}+T C\to i n t e r m e d i a t e s\to C O_{2}+H_{2}O}}\end{array}
