To determine the active sites on the catalyst surface, the corelevel XPS spectra of $\mathtt{C o2p}$ and Fe 2p were analyzed (Fig. S9). After the reaction, the molar ratio of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ is decreased from 5.34 to 5.22, and the value of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ is decreased from 0.56 to 0.32, indicating ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ are tended to convert to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ during the Fenton-like reaction, further confirming the reactions in Eqs. (4) and (5).
## 3.4. Stability and recyclability
The catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ under different solution pH values were investigated. The Fenton-like reaction works well under acidic and neutral environment, and at least $69.5\%$ of TC is effectively degraded within $15~\mathrm{min}$ (Fig. 9a). However, the degradation efficiency is largely suppressed under alkaline condition, and the degradation process is nearly terminated when the solution pH value is higher than 11. A possible reason is that PMS prefers to convert to $S0_{5}^{-}$ , whose oxidability is much less than that of $S O_{4}^{-}$ [39,40].
The influence of anions on degradation efficiency is also studied. Among the common anions, $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{-}$ possesses the most pronounced retard effect, since it usually acts as the scavenger of OH [18,41]. The small pores on PPy shell $(3.0-5.0\ \mathrm{nm})$ provide unimpeded channel for the diffusion of small reactants and products. Nevertheless, the large molecules are blocked outside the shell to prevent the interruption of the Fenton-like reaction inside the cavity. HA $(2.0-3.0~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ [17], a well-known quenching agent for ${}^{1}0_{2}$ , can easily cover on the catalyst surface, leading to the unavailable contact between pollutant and radicals [17,42]. Although $^10_{2}$ acts as the main active species, HA generates little influence on degradation efficiency due to the isolation function of PPy shell (Fig. 9b).
The recyclability was further evaluated. In $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PB}\mathrm{A@\mathrm{PP}}\mathrm{y}-10,$ the two yolks co-exist in PPy shell. By taking advantage of superparamagnetic property, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}-10$ is easily separated from the aqueous solution by a magnet, and then reused in next cycle (Fig. S1). As shown in Fig. 9c, the degradation efficiency of the 4th cycle is $84.5\%$ of the 1st cycle. For comparison, in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/$ PBA-10, the PBA nanocubes and residual $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanospheres are physically mixed together (Fig. S5). Although PBA possessed high activity, the superparamagnetic property from $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ could not be utilized for PBA separation. Therefore, $51.3\%$ of degradation efficiency is lost in the 2nd cycle using $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{PBA}/-10$ as catalyst under the identical condition. In the 3rd cycle, the catalytic activity is nearly completely lost, further confirming the importance of the yolk@shell structure. Besides the recyclability, the morphology and structure of the reclaimed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}-10$ were also tested after Fenton-like reaction, and both the yolk@shell structure and double-yolk are well-preserved (Fig. S10).
Based on the above results, a possible mechanism for Fentonlike reaction on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A}{-}10$ are illustrated in Fig. 10. In comparison with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{PBA}-10$ , TC and
PMS were easy to enrich inside $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ nanoreactor. According to the Eq. (8), when the concentrations of all reactants were increased, the degradation rate was dramatically accelerated. Once the PMS was activated to be $S O_{4}^{-}$ and OH, the concentrations of both TC and radicals were ultra-high due to the limited space in cavity. Therefore, $S0_{4}^{-}$ and OH were tended to convert to ${}^{1}0_{2}$ , resulting in the acceleration of TC degradation. Additionally, the large molecules were blocked outside the shell, and the Fenton-like reaction inside the cavity were performed without interruption, leading to the high environmental adaption.
## 4. Conclusion
In this work, a double-yolk egg-like $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A@P P y}$ nanoreactor was prepared using the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{PPy}$ as precursor via the reactive-template method. Formation mechanism indicated that both HCl concentration and the etching time played important roles for shaping the final structure. By controlling these two factors, pure PBA nanocubes or $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{PBA}$ core@shell composite could also be encapsulated into the PPy shell. In $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy-}$ 10 nanoreactor, the confinement effect from the cavity, the protective effect from the PPy shell, and the magnetic property from the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ were successfully integrated together. Therefore, the concentration of leached cobalt ions was reduced from $1.47~\mathrm{mg/L}$ to $0.174\ \mathrm{mg/L}$ by the protective function from the PPy shell. The nanoreactor could be magnetically separated from the solution, and $84.5\%$ of activity was remained even after the 4th cycle. Most importantly, the degradation rate and mineralization efficiencies of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ were increased to $0.38\ \mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ and $64.7\%$ with the help of confinement effect. The catalytic mechanism revealed that the main active species in $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/P B A}{-}10$ system were OH, while those were $^10_{2}$ in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{PBA@PPy}{-10}$ system due to the confinement effect. This work provided a novel solution to improve degradation efficiency, lower the leached metal ions, and accelerate the catalyst separation in Fenton-like reaction by a targeted design of double-yolk egg-like nanoreactor with multiple functionalities.
